CHROMIUM: esdfs: fix spurious EACCES, dead case-insensitive lookup and
the ACCESS_DISABLE latch

Ports the esdfs fixes made on the 6.12 copy (project-openfyde-patches
065-add-esdfs.patch, commit b30acaa) to the 6.1 esdfs port that
002-chromiumos-fs-mm.patch installs. orangepi5 builds this with
CONFIG_ESD_FS=y, and fydroid's /sdcard is a FUSE mount stacked on these
esdfs views, so every one of these is on the hot path for Android
storage.

1. derive.c, esdfs_set_derived_perms(): the mode was built by
   read-modify-writing inode->i_mode in place ~11 times, starting with
   "i_mode &= S_IFMT" -- i.e. momentarily mode 0000. This runs from
   esdfs_getattr() (every stat) and esdfs_open(), with no lock, on an
   inode other threads are using concurrently. A racing
   esdfs_permission() -> generic_permission() reading i_mode inside that
   window sees no permission bits and returns -EACCES for a file the
   caller is entitled to -- a spurious, load-dependent failure on a
   live, byte-correct file. Build the mode in a local and publish it
   with a single WRITE_ONCE.

2. esdfs.h, esdfs_copy_lower_attr(): same hazard, "dest->i_mode =
   src->i_mode & S_IFMT" also publishes a permission-less mode on a live
   inode. Store (src type | dest perms) in one WRITE_ONCE instead.

3. esdfs.h + file.c: ESDFS_IOC_DIS_ACCESS was a one-way latch. Every
   path that tests ACCESS_DISABLE returns -ENOENT, so once set the whole
   mount looks permanently empty and only umount recovers it. Add the
   counterpart ESDFS_IOC_EN_ACCESS (CAP_SYS_ADMIN).

4. file.c, esdfs_file_llseek(): replayed the seek against the lower file
   after seeking the upper one, so SEEK_CUR applied its delta twice.
   Mirror the resulting f_pos instead. Drop the creds override (nothing
   below touches the lower fs or checks permissions, so it only added a
   GFP_KERNEL allocation and an -ENOMEM failure mode to a hot syscall),
   and refresh the stale cached i_size for the whences that actually
   consult it. The same lower tree is exported through several esdfs
   superblocks, each with its own inode, so a write through one never
   updates the size another has cached. Also install it as
   esdfs_main_fops.llseek, which was still generic_file_llseek.

5. file.c, esdfs_open(): refresh i_size from the lower inode.
   esdfs_copy_attr() deliberately leaves i_size alone, but SEEK_END is
   answered out of it.

6. lookup.c: the filldir_t callback returned the wrong polarity.
   filldir_t returns true to keep iterating and false to stop, but this
   returned false on a mismatch, aborting iterate_dir() at the very
   first entry -- normally "." -- so buf->found was never set and
   esdfs_lookup_nocase() always returned -ENOENT. Case-insensitive
   lookup was dead code. Also bound the comparison by namelen:
   qstr_case_eq() -> strcasecmp() is NUL-terminated and ignores the
   length, but dir_emit() makes no termination guarantee, so it read
   past the entry into inter-dirent padding.

7. lookup.c: the case-insensitive fallback leaked its errno to the
   caller. The exact lookup already proved the name absent, so the only
   useful outcomes are "found a case-variant" or -ENOENT.
   __esdfs_lookup() treats any non-ENOENT error as fatal before it
   reaches the LOOKUP_CREATE rescue, so leaking -ENOMEM, -EIO, -EINTR or
   -EACCES turned "create a .tmp file" into a hard failure.

8. inode.c: log the inputs when generic_permission() denies, and when a
   stuck ACCESS_DISABLE latch reports -ENOENT. stat() claiming a file
   does not exist is indistinguishable from the file really being gone,
   and userspace reacts by deleting and re-fetching it.

Two fixes from the 6.12 commit are deliberately NOT ported:

 - The mmap probe change (a_ops->writepage -> a_ops->writepages) fixes a
   post-folio-conversion defect. 6.1's ext4 still provides .writepage
   (fs/ext4/inode.c), so the existing probe is correct here and there is
   no bug to fix.

 - The splice creds fix does not apply: 6.1 has no backing-file API
   (include/linux/backing-file.h is 6.9+), so this port uses
   generic_file_splice_read/iter_file_splice_write and never had the
   miswritten backing_file_ctx.cred assignment.

